on march 2nd spacex ceo  elon musk announced his plan to create a  new city called  star base in what is now boko chika  village  a tiny unincorporated community of about  30 homes  located next to the southernmost beach  in texas  musk's tweet and his updates encouraging  his followers to move to the brownsville  south padre  area has created a surge of interest in  local real estate  mostly from outside buyers we probably  will not see anything like this  in our lifetime who knows if browns will  ever have an opportunity like this again  we certainly haven't in the past  but while many in the state welcome the  increased investment  and jobs some in south texas worried  that spacex's  expansion will come at a steep price to  the local people  and the environment they in effect have  shut down me personally and economically  they nickeled and dimed us to death  spacex did not respond to multiple  requests  for comment the question now is how will  elon musk build his gateway to mars  in the face of a local backlash  the conflict centers around control of a  state highway  that is the only road to both spacex's  rocket testing site  and a beloved community beach texas  highway 4  which is known locally as bokuchiko  boulevard is a two-lane road  that stretches from the shores of  bokuchika beach to the city of  brownsville  after he did his tweet it really changed  things a lot  craig grove has been a real estate  broker in the brownsville area  for 11 years we get calls like i said  from all over  the united states and even outside the  united states my phone volume i have a  answering service it's probably doubled  or quadrupled  i know of properties that were on the  market  for a good long time that didn't sell  and now because of elon's tweet they  ended up going for  you know cash offers if even a fraction  of what  spacex wants to do happens it's going to  change the face of brownsville  a lot of the people that live in these  houses are spacex engineers  and then if you look behind me over here  this is a brand new subdivision  that's going to be opening up so you can  see  it's happening spacex has had a  footprint in bogochica  since 2012 but the aerospace company  didn't begin ramping up work at the site  until 2019  when it began scaling up development of  its starship spacecraft  starship is the largest playing object  ever  made this is the same vehicle nasa  recently selected to return humans to  the moon  in 2024 best memories of my life are  right here  on this beach now i have to check if  it's okay to go  bobby lerma still lives down the street  from the beach on land  that has been in his family for five  generations  i would like to describe boca chica  beach as a paradise  this is what i want to protect i want my  kids and grandkids to come here  like i did until  recently bocachica which sits at the  edge of a national wildlife refuge  was a favorite spot for locals looking  for a quiet escape  endangered sea turtles and ocelots call  the refuge home  as well as scores of migratory birds but  since spacex began expanding its  operations in 2019  conservationists worry that the eventual  environmental impact  will be devastating  in just a few years the area surrounding  boca chica  has been transformed i have to go  through ask permission to come to my own  home spacex had security of people of  their own  bossing everybody around and requiring  ids  and things that aren't necessary for a  public highway an faa spokesperson  said that in december of 2020 the agency  approved a request by spacex to increase  the closure hours to 300 hours annually  he said implementation and enforcement  of those agreements was a local matter  in texas public access to gulf coast  beaches  is a right enshrined in the state's  constitution  but in 2013 cameron county received a  special exemption from state lawmakers  to temporarily close the beach and the  highway  during rocket launches today some locals  say  this privilege has been abused and that  the frequent highway closures  have essentially privatized the beach in  a phone conversation with the journal  cameron county judge eddie trevino said  we want spacex to succeed but not at the  expense of the community  if they think they'll be able to take  over the highway or the beach they're  mistaken  texas house representative alex  dominguez said he has filed a bill to  limit beach access  closures at boca chica but he is working  with spacex officials to try to improve  beach access  without limiting spacex's allowed  closures  spacex has faced the most friction from  the residents of bogota village  not only did they move in and change the  whole tone and tenor of the place but  but they began to do 24 7 construction  which meant you know wall-to-wall people  cars traffic noise lighting  you name it cheryl stevens was a  homeowner for 15 years in the village  and grew up going to the beach like many  former boca chica village residents  stevens purchased the property thinking  it would be her retirement home  there was no way to stay i mean it was  just so stressful  over the past year multiple spacex  rockets have exploded  breaking windows scattering debris and  causing fires  i mean it was really basically the gun  to the head approach  not a lot of a negotiation on the price  and a lot of  thinly veil threats of eminent domain  steven said she sold to spacex in late  2019  for more than 139 thousand five hundred  dollars  but she characterized the company's  initial offers as unfair  and predatory for his part  bobby lermas said he will continue  speaking out about the road closures  they are kind of pesky at calling me  every day about whether i want to sell  but i'm not going anywhere and i'm not  be bullied by anybody  i'm going to fight back why because it  is my backyard so  i have to protect my backyard because  nobody else will
 

